## Proof

Let
$$S_n=\left(n\prod_{k=2}^{n-1}k^{\,2^{n-k}}\right)^{2^{-n}}.$$
Taking logarithms,
$$\log S_n=2^{-n}\log n+\sum_{k=2}^{n-1}2^{-k}\log k.$$

**Step 1. The boundary term vanishes.** Since $2^{-n}\log n\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$, the first term contributes nothing in the limit.

**Step 2. The series converges.** For $k\ge 2$ we have $0\le 2^{-k}\log k\le k\,2^{-k}$, and $\sum_{k\ge 1}k\,2^{-k}=2<\infty$, so $\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}2^{-k}\log k$ converges absolutely.

**Step 3. Passage to the limit.** Because the series converges, the partial sums satisfy
$$\sum_{k=2}^{n-1}2^{-k}\log k\;\longrightarrow\;\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}2^{-k}\log k.$$
The $k=1$ term equals $2^{-1}\log 1=0$, so we may write
$$\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}2^{-k}\log k=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}2^{-k}\log k.$$

Therefore
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\log S_n=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}2^{-k}\log k.$$

**Step 4. Exponentiate.** By continuity of $\exp$,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}S_n=\exp\!\left(\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}2^{-k}\log k\right)=\prod_{k=1}^{\infty}k^{\,1/2^{k}}.$$

This infinite product converges (its logarithm is the absolutely convergent series above). Numerically,
$$\prod_{k=1}^{\infty}k^{\,1/2^{k}}\approx 1.661687949\ldots$$
which is the **Somos quadratic recurrence constant** $\sigma$.

Hence
$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}S_n=\prod_{k=1}^{\infty}k^{\,1/2^{k}}\approx 1.6617.}$$

